---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { aff } from "../data/affiliates";
import { asset, path } from "../lib/paths";
const pageImage = asset("images/hero.jpg");

const faq = [
  {
    q: "How much is a taxi from Tbilisi Airport to the city center?",
    a: "Bolt or Yandex Go usually cost 30–40 GEL. The official TBS Taxi charges a fixed 40 GEL for destinations up to 19.9 km from the airport, which covers the center.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does bus 337 run 24 hours?",
    a: "No. It leaves the airport roughly between 07:00 and 23:00. For a night arrival, use Bolt, TBS Taxi or a pre-booked transfer.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I pay for the bus with cash?",
    a: "It is better to tap a contactless bank card or phone. Cash is accepted on some buses only, and a cash fare doesn't include the 90 minutes of free transfers.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is there a train from Tbilisi Airport?",
    a: "The airport has a railway station, but trains were not running as of November 2026.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the metro go to the airport?",
    a: "No. Bus 337 stops at Avlabari and Isani metro stations, and you can transfer there within the same 90-minute fare.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long does it take to get from the airport to the city?",
    a: "About 25–30 minutes by car and about an hour by bus.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Tbilisi Airport to City Center (2026): Bus 337, Bolt, Taxi & Transfer Prices | ArriveGE"
  description="Every way from Tbilisi Airport (TBS) to the city center: bus 337 for 1 GEL, Bolt for 30–40 GEL, fixed-fare taxi and pre-booked transfers. Prices checked November 2026."
  nextHref="/tbilisi-hotels"
  nextLabel="Tbilisi hotels"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqSchema} />

  <header class="page-hero has-photo" style={`--page-image: url('${pageImage}')`}>
    <div class="page-hero-inner" data-reveal>
      <p class="section-kicker">TBS → city</p>
      <h1>Tbilisi Airport to City Center</h1>
      <p>
        The airport is about 17 km from the center. Most travelers should take Bolt for 30–40 GEL (25–30 minutes). On a
        tight budget, bus 337 costs 1 GEL and takes about an hour.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="section content-grid">
    <article class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>Quick answer: which option is right for you?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cheapest:</strong> bus 337, 1 GEL, runs roughly 07:00–23:00.</li>
        <li><strong>Best value door to door:</strong> Bolt, 30–40 GEL. You need mobile data to book.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Landing late at night, or no mobile data:</strong> a pre-booked transfer or the airport's fixed-fare TBS
          Taxi (40 GEL).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Family or lots of luggage:</strong> a pre-booked transfer or Bolt XL (up to 6 seats).</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid:</strong> drivers who walk up to you in the arrivals hall offering a ride.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Prices last checked: November 2026.</em></p>

      <h2>All options compared</h2>
      <table class="compare">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Option</th>
            <th>Price</th>
            <th>Time</th>
            <th>Hours</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Bus 337</td>
            <td>1 GEL (about 1.50 GEL on a foreign card)</td>
            <td>~60 min</td>
            <td>~07:00–23:00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Bolt / Yandex Go</td>
            <td>30–40 GEL</td>
            <td>25–30 min</td>
            <td>24/7</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>TBS Taxi (official, fixed fare)</td>
            <td>40 GEL up to 19.9 km, 50 GEL beyond</td>
            <td>25–30 min</td>
            <td>24/7</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pre-booked transfer</td>
            <td>from ~55 GEL</td>
            <td>25–30 min</td>
            <td>24/7</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Train</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td>Not running (Nov 2026)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Prices in Georgian lari (GEL). Ride-hailing fares rise at night and when demand is high.</p>

      <h2>Bus 337: the 1 GEL option</h2>
      <p>Bus 337 is the cheapest way into Tbilisi. It runs from the airport to the central railway station through the city center.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Find the stop.</strong> Walk out of the arrivals hall. The municipal bus stop is right in front of the terminal.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pay by tapping your card.</strong> Tap a contactless bank card or phone wallet when you board. A Georgian
          card pays 1 GEL; foreign cards are charged about 1.50 GEL. The fare covers 90 minutes of unlimited rides, so a
          transfer to the metro is included. There is no need to tap off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Optional: get a transport card.</strong> The blue card costs 2 GEL at the Bank of Georgia counter in
          arrivals. It is worth it only if you plan to use day passes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Get off at the right stop.</strong> Freedom Square / Pushkin Park for the Old Town and Sololaki; Avlabari
          metro for Avlabari and Metekhi; Station Square for the central railway station.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Buses leave the airport roughly between 07:00 and 23:00, every 15–20 minutes. The ride takes about an hour, longer
        at rush hour.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tip:</strong> Parts of Rustaveli Avenue were closed to traffic in the second half of 2026 and the bus was
        diverted. Check the live route in Google Maps before you go.
      </p>

      <h2>Bolt: the easiest way for most travelers</h2>
      <p>
        Bolt is the main ride-hailing app in Tbilisi. You see the fare before you confirm, and the driver is tracked. From
        the airport to the center it usually costs 30–40 GEL.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Set it up before you fly.</strong> Install Bolt and verify your phone number at home. Verification by SMS can fail on arrival.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Have mobile data ready.</strong> Airport Wi-Fi is often too weak to match you with a driver. An eSIM
          bought in advance saves the hassle —
          <a href={aff("esim")} target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored noreferrer">get an eSIM for Georgia</a>.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Where to meet the driver.</strong> Exit the terminal. The pickup area is right in front, next to the bus stop.</li>
        <li><strong>Pay by card or cash.</strong> Link a card in the app or pay the driver in lari.</li>
        <li><strong>Traveling with a group?</strong> Choose Bolt XL for up to 6 people.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Yandex Go also works in Tbilisi and is often a little cheaper. It is worth comparing both apps.</p>

      <h2>Pre-booked transfer: worth it at night and with family</h2>
      <p>
        A pre-booked transfer costs from about 55 GEL. The price is fixed when you book, and the driver waits for you in
        arrivals with a name sign. Book with your flight number so the driver tracks delays.
      </p>
      <p>It is worth the extra money when:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>your flight lands between midnight and 06:00, when the buses are not running;</li>
        <li>you travel with children, a car seat or a lot of luggage;</li>
        <li>you have no mobile data on arrival;</li>
        <li>you are going straight to Kazbegi or Kakheti;</li>
        <li>you want a fixed price with no surprises after a long flight.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a solo traveler landing in the daytime with mobile data, Bolt is usually cheaper.
        <a href={aff("transfer")} target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored noreferrer">Compare transfer prices for your date</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Mistakes to avoid on arrival</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Taking a ride from drivers who approach you.</strong> Drivers in the arrivals hall often ask 60 GEL or
          more, sometimes far more, and some even claim that Bolt is not working. Book through the app or use the official
          fixed-fare TBS Taxi.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Getting into a car that doesn't match the app.</strong> Check the plate number and the driver's name against Bolt before you get in.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Changing all your money at the airport.</strong> Airport exchange rates are worse than in the city. Change
          only what you need for the first day. US dollars usually get a better rate than euros.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Using a machine that isn't an ATM.</strong> The payment terminals (payboxes) after customs do not give out
          cash. Look for bank ATMs instead: Bank of Georgia (orange) charges a percentage fee, TBC (light blue) a flat 5 GEL.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Overpaying for a SIM card.</strong> The Magti desk in arrivals is open 24 hours, but airport tourist
          packages cost more than in the city. If you only need data, an eSIM set up before your flight is easier —
          <a href={path("esim-georgia")}>see our eSIM guide</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      {
        faq.map((f) => (
          <>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </article>

    <aside class="action-panel" data-reveal>
      <h2>Book a driver</h2>
      <p>Fixed price, meet at arrivals, flight tracking. Compare the live quote for your flight date.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={aff("transfer")} target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored noreferrer"
        >Book airport transfer</a
      >
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={path("esim-georgia")}>Need eSIM first?</a>
      <p class="disclaimer">Affiliate disclosure: we may earn a commission if you book through these links.</p>
    </aside>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
